 "coverage": "79 ICs and 259 nets over 922 pins, from DICE's netlist of the game. DICE is an emulator: its netlist is the circuit as it simulates it, at the board positions the drawings use, and is not a drawing of the board. Where it stands an RC delay or a speed hack in a wire, the wire is restored; what it models that is not a chip on the board (CLOCK_14_318_MHZ, COIN_INPUT, DIODE_MATRIX, DIPSWITCH, JOYSTICK1_INPUT, JOYSTICK2_INPUT, LATCH, MIXER, …) is left off the board view, and the resistors and capacitors are not named at all. Supply pins are joined to +5V and GND from each part's pinout. The ICs sit at their board positions, on the grid spacing of Atari's boards.",
